# Line endings. Every text file is LF, in the index and in every checkout, whatever a host's
# `core.autocrlf` says. A Windows checkout otherwise writes CRLF, and three things then break: bash
# reads the `\r` as part of a command, a check that compares bytes (the licence directory, a
# vendored file) fails on content that is identical, and a file a tool rewrites comes back as a
# whole-file diff. Python on Windows writes CRLF in text mode unless told `newline="\n"`, so a
# script edit is the usual way a CRLF file appears; `cargo xtask check-line-endings` refuses one.
* text=auto eol=lf

# CRLF here is the format, not an accident, and normalising it would break what these describe.
# RFC 5322 mail and RFC 6350 vCards end their lines CRLF, and the iMIP fixtures are real messages,
# parsed by crates/mailcal-app's invitation tests.
*.eml   -text
*.vcf   -text

# The Windows launcher Gradle ships. Left alone rather than declared `text eol=crlf`: that would
# store it LF and rewrite the vendored blob, and the bytes here are Gradle's, not ours.
*.bat   -text
